# FortiGate objects

## Gate

Use when the user asks to list or create address objects, service objects,
or virtual IPs (VIP / DNAT / port-forward). Skip when the ask is only
policy list/status with no object work. Always load `fortigate-safety` for
creates/updates/deletes.

Never invent object names, VIP names, or mapped addresses. Resolve from
MCP or user paste.

## Prefer MCP

When a FortiGate MCP is ready (`user-mcp_fortigate`), use:

| Need | Tool | Notes |
| --- | --- | --- |
| List addresses | `list_address_objects` | `device_id`, optional `vdom` |
| Create address | `create_address_object` | `name`, `address_type`, `address` |
| List services | `list_service_objects` | `device_id`, optional `vdom` |
| Create service | `create_service_object` | `name`, `service_type`, `protocol`, optional `port` |
| List VIPs | `list_virtual_ips` | `device_id`, optional `vdom` |
| VIP detail | `get_virtual_ip_detail` | `device_id` + `name` |
| Create VIP | `create_virtual_ip` | extip/mappedip/extintf (+ portforward fields) |
| Update VIP | `update_virtual_ip` | `name` + `vip_data` |
| Delete VIP | `delete_virtual_ip` | destructive — explicit verb |

## Address objects

1. `list_address_objects` before creating a near-duplicate name.
2. `create_address_object` requires `address_type` and `address` as the
   MCP schema defines — prefer types the live schema/`get_schema_info`
   documents; do not invent exotic type strings.
3. Prefer reusing an existing object in policy craft over minting a new
   one with a conflicting name.
4. Receipt: device, vdom, name, type, address value (CIDR/FQDN as given —
   not credentials).

## Service objects

1. `list_service_objects` before create.
2. `create_service_object`: set `protocol` and `port` when the user named
   a port/range; do not open "all" unless explicitly asked.
3. Prefer built-in service names when listing shows them and they match
   the ask.

## Virtual IPs (NAT)

1. `list_virtual_ips` / `get_virtual_ip_detail` before update/delete.
2. `create_virtual_ip` needs `name`, `extip`, `mappedip`, `extintf`.
   Optional port-forward: `portforward`, `protocol`, `extport`,
   `mappedport`.
3. Resolve `extintf` from `list_interfaces` — never invent WAN interface
   names.
4. Update via `update_virtual_ip` with the smallest `vip_data` change.
5. Delete requires an explicit verb; state that DNAT for that VIP stops.

## Mutate gates

Creates/updates/deletes of address, service, or VIP objects need an
explicit verb this turn (`fortigate-safety`). Do not remove objects still
referenced by live policy without the user naming that consequence.

## Report shape

- Device id + VDOM
- Object kind + name
- Key fields (address/CIDR, port, extip→mappedip)
- For mutates: created/updated/deleted + blast note

## Out of scope

- Firewall policy row authoring (see `fortigate-policies`)
- Static routes (see `fortigate-routes`)
- Site VIP numbering plans — take addresses from the user or inventory
